  "answer": "The study found 4 of the 5 cases it was asked for, so the goal was not met. Each of these settings failed on every repeat, and its range sits above one half: 023b7fcc with the cars 1.0 s later at 1.5x speed (S3), 096988dd at 0.5 s earlier and 0.75x (S9), 0e899dd3 at 0 s and 2.0x (S20), and 1c45ce3a at 2.0 s later and 1.0x (S31). In three of them (S3, S9, S20) the policy caused the crash: it drifted or changed lanes to the left against its route command and hit, or was hit by, a car in that lane. The fourth (S31) passes the check but is weak evidence against the policy. In every run there, the ego stayed on the recorded path, and the replayed cut-in car came up from behind and side-swiped it. The triage did not blame the policy for any of those runs, so this case mostly shows that replayed traffic can't react. Each confirmed setting rests on only a few repeats, so all we know is that its failure rate is above one half, not how far above. No fifth scene broke.",
